WEBVTT
TITLE Readers Writers And Dining Philosophers — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
One shared value, two kinds of user: many readers, one writer.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Three readers inside together is correct. A writer inside with them is not.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Five philosophers, five forks, and each needs the two beside him.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
One problem is about fairness. The other is about a cycle.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Three rules: a writer is alone, readers share, and nobody starves for ever.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
One mutex around the whole section: correct, and needlessly serial.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
The bars on the right: three readers serialised, then three readers overlapping.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
So the design question is: who decides, and when? A counter can decide.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Two semaphores and one integer: rmutex protects the count, wsem protects the data.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Reader one: count 0 to 1, and because she is first she takes wsem.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Reader two: count 1 to 2, and she never touches wsem. Both read together.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
A writer now blocks on wsem at minus one, and the last reader will wake him.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Readers keep arriving. Each new reader skips the wait on wsem.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Count four, wsem still minus one: four readers in, one writer out.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
If readers never all leave, the writer never enters. That is starvation.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
This is called reader preference, and it is a design choice, not a bug in the code.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Add one gate, readTry, that every reader must pass through.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
One reader inside, then a writer: the writer takes readTry and blocks on wmutex.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Reader two now blocks on readTry at minus one: the gate is shut.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Second problem: writers first. Third problem: nobody starves, and it costs more.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
How many processes can be inside? Readers: all of them. Writers: one.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
How many semaphores? Two for the first solution, four for the second.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
A negative value is a queue length. Minus one means one process waiting.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Who may barge, and what each solution guarantees, in one table.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Five philosophers, five forks, and a loop of think, pick up, eat, put down.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
All five take the left fork at the same instant. Every fork is now held.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Each now waits for a fork his neighbour holds. Nothing can move.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Four conditions, all true here: exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, a cycle.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
A philosopher is in one of four situations, so the whole table is finite.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Search every reachable state: five hundred and seventy-three of them.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Exactly one state has no legal move, and it is all five holding their left fork.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
The same search says at most two can eat: two meals need four forks.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Fix one: a room semaphore with four permits, so one philosopher waits outside.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Fix two: break the symmetry, so the odd philosophers reach right first.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Fix three: a waiter that grants both forks or neither, as a monitor does.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Every repair removes one condition, and none of them removes waiting.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Deadlock: nothing moves. Starvation: one class never chosen. They differ.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Bounded waiting is the third promise, and it is the one usually broken.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Read the code in this order: which variable is shared, what guards it, who signals.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Both problems are one idea: a resource that two rules disagree about.

